<div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-12 sm:py-16">
	<header class="max-w-2xl">
		<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Designing Cubby by living with it</h2>
		<p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed">
			I use Cubby every day, which means I keep running into my own decisions. Some things I liked when I built them
			became annoying quite quickly. Others took longer to notice, especially when I was the one who knew how everything
			worked.
		</p>
	</header>
	<div class="mt-10 max-w-2xl">
		<div>
			<section id="everyday-use" class="scroll-mt-28 pb-16 last:pb-0">
				<h3 class="border-neutral/30 border-t pt-6 text-3xl leading-snug font-bold">What I reach for every day</h3>
				<section id="daily-actions" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">Why Home is crowded</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							I log tasks and limits several times a day, so opening their pages each time became tedious. I wanted the
							things I did every day within one scroll and one interaction, even if that meant Home was a bit crowded.
						</p>
						<p>
							That meant some duplication. For a Limit, I wanted enough state to understand the allowance and a quick
							way to add to it. History and configuration could stay on the Limits page. Tasks on Home could help me act
							on what needed attention, while the Tasks page kept the larger working set.
						</p>
						<p>
							I could have made Home a summary with links into each feature, but then I'd keep paying for that tidy
							structure every time I wanted to log something. I accepted a denser dashboard because I wanted to do the
							common things where I already was.
						</p>
					</div>
					<a class="mt-5 inline-block py-2 text-sm font-semibold underline underline-offset-4" href="#home-design"
						>See how Home evolved</a
					>
				</section>
				<section id="shared-hub" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">
						I tried to put too much into the same interaction
					</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							I kept coming back to the idea of a common hub for Tasks, Limits and the other logs. They had quite a lot
							in common underneath, especially timestamps and histories, so it seemed like there should be a way to
							bring them together. Each attempt mostly felt like I'd put several features next to each other.
						</p>
						<p>
							Logging a drink takes almost no thought, but a Task might need me to decide whether to do it now or leave
							it. With an illness, I might want to write quite a bit about what happened.
						</p>
						<p>
							A Limit also needs to show how the week is going after I add something. An illness entry might need a
							photo or a medicine dose. I still wanted to reuse code where it helped. I think the shared schema fooled
							me into thinking the UI should be shared too.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section id="frequency" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">More space for stuff I actually use</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							At one point, my rough order of use was Tasks, then Limits, Meals, Prices, Illness, Expiry, Gym and
							Growing Up. That order can change, but there's a long tail of features I use much less often.
						</p>
						<p>
							I had to get used to giving them uneven amounts of space. Something could be important and still not
							deserve a permanent Home card. Meanwhile, something completely mundane could deserve a spot right there
							because I did it several times a day.
						</p>
						<p>
							Tasks was a useful example. I used it a lot, but because I could do so much from Home, I had less reason
							to open the Tasks page itself. Another feature might deserve a navigation slot simply because I actually
							needed to open its page.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>
			</section>
			<section id="domain-decisions" class="scroll-mt-28 pb-16 last:pb-0">
				<h3 class="border-neutral/30 border-t pt-6 text-3xl leading-snug font-bold">
					Where the data model stopped helping
				</h3>
				<section id="natural-capture" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">Just wanted to write a note</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							I had structured symptoms in the illness tracker because I thought they'd be useful to compare later. But
							when my son was unwell, I often just wanted to record what I'd noticed. Something like a small ulcer under
							his tongue the night before, then a painful sore on his foot in the morning, although he otherwise seemed
							well.
						</p>
						<p>
							Choosing symptom categories felt like too much work. I could write down what I saw without having to
							decide exactly what to call it. Temperature and medicine timings were different because those were already
							specific things I wanted to record.
						</p>
						<p>
							A photo could help me see how something had changed, while a doctor's note kept what had been assessed or
							advised. Those were useful things to capture in their own forms. Structured symptoms might still be useful
							later. But when my son was unwell, categorizing everything while I was trying to record what happened was
							just annoying.
						</p>
						<p>
							So I made Note the first action in Add an update, with structured symptoms still available alongside it. I
							could write the observation down immediately and use the more specific inputs when I needed them.
						</p>
					</div>
					<a class="mt-5 inline-block py-2 text-sm font-semibold underline underline-offset-4" href="#health"
						>See the illness feature</a
					>
				</section>
				<section id="sleep-domain" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">Sleepwalking needed somewhere of its own</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							When I wanted to record sleepwalking, I already had Tasks and Illness in Cubby. It would have been
							convenient to put the episode into whichever existing feature was closest, but neither felt like a
							convincing fit. An unusual observation didn't automatically belong in an illness record.
						</p>
						<p>
							A generic event log would have lost the sleep context too. I wanted to keep an episode with the night it
							happened, alongside sleep and wake times. Naps and patterns across nights also made more sense together,
							so Sleep got its own model.
						</p>
						<p>
							That still left a separate question about where it should appear. Sleep could have its own proper record
							without automatically earning a card on Home or a prime navigation slot.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section id="task-grouping" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">Task headings had to help me decide what to do</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							Tasks could have been divided up according to their dates and statuses. But when I opened the page, I was
							usually working out what to deal with soon and what I'd already let slip. The things I could leave until
							later needed somewhere to sit too.
						</p>
						<p>
							I settled on Today &amp; Tomorrow, Delayed, and Backlog. Those headings did some of the interpretation I'd
							otherwise have to do each time I scanned the dates. I could see the kind of decision I needed to make
							before reading every item.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section id="timeline-role" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">Timeline helped me get my bearings</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							Time was a connection I actually wanted across Cubby. Medicine timings could sit near illness
							observations, and a day could include meals as well as a workout. I wanted to see what had just happened
							and what was coming up, which gave Timeline a purpose that the common hub had struggled to find.
						</p>
						<p>
							I considered making it the main screen, but chronology couldn't decide everything that deserved my
							attention. An overdue Task or a frequently used Limit could matter more than whichever event happened to
							come next.
						</p>
						<p>
							I kept a compact Timeline on Home, with a full view when I wanted more detail. It tells me roughly where
							we are in the day without turning Home into a chronological feed.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>
			</section>
			<section id="dashboard-design" class="scroll-mt-28 pb-16 last:pb-0">
				<h3 class="border-neutral/30 border-t pt-6 text-3xl leading-snug font-bold">
					Making room without making a mess
				</h3>
				<section id="surface-hierarchy" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">Everything started to look like a card</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							For a while, a card was the obvious way to contain each thing on Home. It separated the information neatly
							and made a component easy to move around. But once I had enough of them, the white rounded cards on tinted
							sections started to look like stickers on coloured paper.
						</p>
						<p>
							Each card seemed reasonable on its own. Together, they gave too many things the same weight. A small
							metric could sit directly on its section background, while denser information or a group of related
							actions still benefited from a container.
						</p>
						<p>
							So I stopped giving everything its own white box. Related stuff could sit together, and the things that
							actually needed a container could keep one.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section id="responsive-layout" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">Mobile needed a different arrangement</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							Limits made this obvious. On desktop, several small items could sit beside each other and save a lot of
							vertical space. Trying to carry that same arrangement onto a phone made each item cramped, with awkward
							wrapping that slowed down scanning.
						</p>
						<p>
							I increasingly preferred a single reading flow on mobile and used the extra width on desktop. The item
							still needed to be recognizable, and its action had to behave the same way, but its position didn't need
							to prove that it was the same component.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section id="contextual-groups" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">
						Household became a category for almost everything
					</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							Household started as a useful group, then kept absorbing things because so much of Cubby could plausibly
							go there. Eventually the label told me very little about what I'd find inside it.
						</p>
						<p>
							Tasks and Limits were awkward in a different way because they could apply to almost any part of life. I
							tried groups based on the person, the domain and the purpose of the information, but none gave me a
							taxonomy that worked everywhere.
						</p>
						<p>
							Eventually I stopped trying to find one taxonomy that worked everywhere. Home needed groups that were easy
							to scan, while navigation needed groups that helped me find stuff. The database could organize the same
							records differently again.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section id="home-customization" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">Pin was a Home setting in the wrong place</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							I initially put a pin control on individual Limits because it seemed convenient to choose one right there.
							But it became unclear what the pin meant, particularly when the same Limit appeared in different places.
							Was I changing something in Limits or choosing what appeared on Home?
						</p>
						<p>
							What I wanted was to choose the Limits in my Home quick log, so I moved that into Customize on the Home
							section. That gave the setting a clear place to live and let the individual tiles go back to showing the
							allowance and the add button. I could also remove the old pin behavior from the shared component, which
							was one less thing to keep around.
						</p>
					</div>
					<a class="mt-5 inline-block py-2 text-sm font-semibold underline underline-offset-4" href="#home-design"
						>See the current Home model</a
					>
				</section>
				<section id="clear-controls" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">Liked the dock until I kept misclicking it</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							I liked the floating dock when I built it, but I kept misclicking it in actual use. Eventually I went back
							to a conventional navigation bar with labels, which worked much better. It was a bit annoying to give up
							something I'd enjoyed building.
						</p>
						<p>
							I ran into a similar problem when I made create buttons more compact. New task and Add flight became less
							obvious when I replaced the words with generic icons, so I restored the labels. Moving a page title beside
							the back button was a better way to save space because I could reclaim a row and still read everything.
						</p>
						<p>
							A back arrow can stay compact because its meaning is familiar. I'd taken that too far with actions that
							actually needed words. Now if a label doesn't fit, I try fixing the layout before deleting the label.
						</p>
					</div>
					<a class="mt-5 inline-block py-2 text-sm font-semibold underline underline-offset-4" href="#home-design"
						>See the current Home model</a
					>
				</section>
			</section>
			<section id="visual-feedback" class="scroll-mt-28 pb-16 last:pb-0">
				<h3 class="border-neutral/30 border-t pt-6 text-3xl leading-snug font-bold">How it felt after repeated use</h3>
				<section id="surface-colours" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">
						Colours matched in code but looked wrong together
					</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							Using one colour at different opacities seemed like a tidy way to build the palette. Once I had tinted
							sections and cards, though, that colour was blending against several different backgrounds.
						</p>
						<p>
							I ended up with intermediate shades that looked washed out and slightly dirty. There was no single
							obviously wrong colour, but the whole page had lost some crispness. The variables were consistent while
							the colours I actually saw weren't.
						</p>
						<p>
							So I started choosing the actual surface colour I wanted instead of insisting everything come from one
							neat opacity formula.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section id="repeated-feedback" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">Small responses mattered alot</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							When I add to a Limit, the moving fill shows me that it registered. Haptic feedback helps too, especially
							when I'm logging something while doing something else and would otherwise have to check the screen again.
						</p>
						<p>
							Some completions get a brief star effect, and a bigger moment can have confetti. I initially thought of
							these as polish, but using the same quick actions several times a day made an ambiguous response more
							annoying than I'd expected. Just felt nicer.
						</p>
						<p>
							The filled squircle is a good example. Watching the water move connects the action to the allowance I have
							left, without another message to read. That made the animation useful even before I considered whether I
							liked how it looked.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section id="quiet-feedback" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">Had to be more careful with the celebrations</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							But recording my kid's temperature would be a strange moment for confetti. The save had succeeded, but
							someone was still sick. The feedback system now has separate levels, with confetti reserved for
							milestones. An ordinary save can confirm that the record went through without a celebration.
						</p>

						<p>
							I also wanted to stop short of points and streaks. There's already enough to keep up with at home, and I
							wanted the small interactions to feel good without giving us another score to maintain.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>
			</section>
			<section id="another-user" class="scroll-mt-28 pb-16 last:pb-0">
				<h3 class="border-neutral/30 border-t pt-6 text-3xl leading-snug font-bold">
					Someone else has to want to use it
				</h3>
				<section id="useful-to-others" class="scroll-mt-28 pt-9 pb-6">
					<h4 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold text-balance">My wife still says, “You can do it for me”</h4>
					<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
						<p>
							My wife sometimes says, “You can do it for me.” That's fair enough because we share a household and I can
							record it for her. But if she keeps finding it easier to tell me than to open Cubby, I have to take that
							seriously.
						</p>
						<p>
							I'm a fairly forgiving user of my own app because I know where everything is and why I built it. I already
							think the information is worth recording. She might find the process troublesome, or she might just get
							less out of the record than I do.
						</p>
						<p>
							I'm still figuring that part out. Making a button clearer might help with the first problem, but if
							keeping Cubby up to date mainly benefits me, it's understandable that she'd rather leave it to me.
						</p>
					</div>
				</section>
			</section>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>
